<body>
<header><h1>🔄 Database Transactions</h1><p>Learn how databases handle groups of operations.</p></header>
<main>
<section class="card"><h2>What Is a Transaction?</h2><p>A transaction is a group of database operations treated as one logical unit of work.</p></section>
<section class="card"><div class="letter">A — Atomicity</div><p>All operations in a transaction succeed together, or the transaction is rolled back.</p></section>
<section class="card"><div class="letter">C — Consistency</div><p>A transaction should preserve the database's defined rules and constraints.</p></section>
<section class="card"><div class="letter">I — Isolation</div><p>Concurrent transactions are controlled so they do not interfere in unacceptable ways.</p></section>
<section class="card"><div class="letter">D — Durability</div><p>After a transaction is committed, its changes are designed to persist even after a failure.</p></section>
<section class="card"><h2>Example: Bank Transfer</h2><div class="example"><p>1. Subtract ₹500 from Account A.</p><p>2. Add ₹500 to Account B.</p><p>3. Commit both operations if successful; otherwise, roll back as appropriate.</p></div></section>
</main>
</body>
